# Tailoring Low‐Dimensional Perovskites Passivation for Efficient Two‐Step‐Processed FAPbI3 Solar Cells and Modules

## abstract
Converting PbI2 residues into low‐dimensional perovskites through post‐treatment with ammonium‐based large cations can passivate 3D perovskites, thus has emerged as an effective strategy to improve the performance of perovskite solar cells (PSCs). Herein, a dramatically improved efficiency is demonstrated for PSCs based on a two‐step‐processed FAPbI3 perovskite via post‐treatment with formamidinium (FA)‐based benzamidine hydrochloride (PFACl), outperforming the commonly used methylamine (MA)‐based benzylamine hydrochloride (PMACl). With an in‐depth exploration of the crystal structures and morphology changes of the FAPbI3 perovskite upon the PFACl post‐treatment, the preferential formation of 1D rather than 2D structures on the 3D perovskite film is identified. In contrast to the 2D counterpart, the more energetically favorable 1D structure enables a more effective elimination of PbI2 residues. As a consequence, the PFACl‐induced 1D/3D perovskite film is endowed with smoother morphology, more uniform surface potential distribution, lower trap density, faster charge transfer, and better film stability than the PMACl‐induced 2D/3D perovskite and control films, demonstrating champion efficiencies of 24.9% for a small‐size PSC, 23.6% for a 1 cm2 large‐size PSC, and 21.2% for a 5 × 5 cm2 mini‐module, which is the highest among the perovskite solar mini‐modules using the two‐step deposition method.

Converting $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ residues into low-dimensional perovskites through post-treatment with ammonium-based large cations can passivate 3D perovskites, thus has emerged as an effective strategy to improve the performance of perovskite solar cells (PSCs). Herein, a dramatically improved efficiency is demonstrated for PSCs based on a two-step-processed $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ perovskite via post-treatment with formamidinium (FA)-based benzamidine hydrochloride (PFACl), outperforming the commonly used methylamine (MA)-based benzylamine hydrochloride (PMACl). With an in-depth exploration of the crystal structures and morphology changes of the $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ perovskite upon the PFACl post-treatment, the preferential formation of 1D rather than 2D structures on the 3D perovskite film is identified. In contrast to the 2D counterpart, the more energetically favorable 1D structure enables a more effective elimination of $\left.\mathsf{P b l}_{2}\right.$ residues. As a consequence, the PFACl-induced $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{\overline{{\Pi}}D/3D}}$ perovskite film is endowed with smoother morphology, more uniform surface potential distribution, lower trap density, faster charge transfer, and better film stability than the PMACl-induced 2D/3D perovskite and control films, demonstrating champion efficiencies of $24.9\%$ for a small-size PSC, $23.6\%$ for a $\mathsf{1c m}^{2}$ large-size PSC, and $21.2\%$ for a $5\times5c m^{2}$ mini-module, which is the highest among the perovskite solar mini-modules using the two-step deposition method.
## 1. Introduction
Organic-inorganic hybrid metal halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have shown great competitiveness in next-generation photovoltaics due to the incomparable merits of excellent photovoltaic performance and low production cost.[1–3] The up-to-date highest certified power conversion efficiency (PCE) of smallsized PSCs has reached $26.1\%$ ,[4] while the PCEs of large-scale perovskite solar modules (PSMs) are still far behind.[5–8] Toward further commercialization, the optimization of the large-area perovskite films undoubtedly constitutes one of the key determinants.
In order to achieve large-scale PSMs with high efficiencies, the optimization of scalable deposition techniques and the development of effective defect passivation strategies of perovskite films are of great significance.[5] The two-step deposition method is a widely used strategy to achieve high-performance PSCs, which is not only applicable for vapor deposition of conformal perovskite films on different substrates,[9,10] but also suitable for large-scale printing production.[11,12] However, the reported PCEs of large-scale PSMs based on the two-step method are still much lower than that of the one-step method.[3,13–17] The most likely main reason is that the predeposited $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ layer fails to react with the subsequent deposited organic salts in a complete and homogeneous way, resulting in a large amount of excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ This phenomenon would be more significant in large-area perovskite films, as the larger the area, the harder the reaction would be.[17] In general, the residual $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is known to be a double-edged sword for PSCs, which could enhance efficiency but sacrifice its longterm stability.[18,19] Therefore, the rational tailoring and exerting positive roles of a secondary $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ phase are of great significance, especially for the large-scale two-step-processed perovskite photovoltaics in which abundant $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ residues are inevitable.[17]
Recently, an increasing number of works have explored strategies to eliminate the negative impacts of excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ including precursor ink engineering,[14] deposition method regulation,[20–22] and interface post-treatment engineering.[23] In particular, the use of large-size cationic halide salts such as the linear alkylammonium halide salts, the phenyl and/or other functional group-based alkylammonium halide salts to convert $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ residues into low-dimensional (LD) perovskites has become a widely used and effective strategy to enhance the performance of the corresponding solar cells. For example, the linear alkylammonium-based halides such as butylammonium,[24] octylammonium,[25] dodecylammonium[26] cationic salts, etc., the phenyl-group and thiophene-group based halide salts such as the phenylethylammonium,[27] 2- thiophenemethylammonium[28] based materials, etc., were all demonstrated to form a 2D structure perovskite for passivating the 3D perovskite films, while some of the large cationic halide salts induce a 1D structure such as the 4-chlorobenzamidine,[29] 2-diethylaminoethylchloride[30] based halide salts, etc. were also demonstrated the effective passivating effects. Nevertheless, there still seems to be a lack of general guidance on how to select LD perovskites for effective passivation of the two-step processed 3D perovskites, especially for systematic comparison and in-depth understanding of the dimensional-reduction mechanism underlying different post-treatment agents or functional groups, as well as their effects on the device performance.
In general, the LD perovskites with a lower formation energy could enable the fast reaction between cationic halides and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ residues, thus managing well the surface physicochemical properties. Herein, we demonstrate this by simply changing the functional groups of the widely used phenyl-based large cations. By replacing the methylamine (MA) group with the formamidine (FA) group on the benzene ring, the much lower formation energy of benzamidine hydrochloride (PFACl)-induced 1D structure forms, and the corresponding post-treated two-step-processed perovskite films exhibit lower surface roughness, narrower surface potential distribution, lower trap density, faster charge transfer, and better stability compared with the post-treatment of benzylamine chloride (PMACl)-induced 2D structure. We propose that the lower formation energy of the LD structured perovskite is able to fast and adequately convert the residual $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ fragment, thus benefiting the uniformity of large-area perovskite films. As a result, the highest PCE of the PFACl-based PSCs achieved $24.4\%$ with a steady-state power output (SPO) of $23.1\%$ . In addition, the champion PCEs of $24.9\%$ and $23.6\%$ were achieved for the optimized small-size and $1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ large-size $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ -based PSCs without hysteresis, along with significantly enhanced stability. More importantly, this optimized post-treatment strategy with PFACl can be easily upscaled to $5\times5\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ PSMs, demonstrating a champion PCE of $21.2\%$ with an SPO of $20.0\%$ . This work reveals the passivation effect of the functional group changes on perovskites, demonstrating an alternative strategy for pursuing efficient and stable large-scale perovskite photovoltaics.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Dimensional Structure Evolution
Figure 1a depicts the two-step sequential deposition of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite films and the subsequent post-treatment using large cationic salts, which give rise to the formation of an LD structure on top of 3D perovskite (Figure 1b). To explore the dimensional evolution of $3\mathrm{D-FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite upon PMACl and PFACl posttreatments, GIWAXS measurements were performed as shown in Figure 1c. In the 3D control film, an out-of-plane diffraction ring at ${\approx}0.9\mathring\mathrm{A}^{-1}$ attributing to the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ residues is visible, indicating a preferential parallel orientation of the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ fragments with respect to the 3D perovskite layer. However, such a stacking mode is unfavorable for stability and tends to trigger the hysteresis effect.[31] As expected, the post-treatment with a certain amount of PMACl induces the formation of 2D structures, as manifested by the emergence of diffraction rings at ${\approx}0.38$ and $0.76\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ in 2D GIWAXS data. Nevertheless, the excessive $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ phase still exists. In contrast, the PFACl treatment significantly diminishes the scattering intensity from $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ but contributes to the appearance of a new peak at ${\approx}0.55\mathrm{\mathring{A}}^{-1}$ (Figure 1d). In order to determine the structure of this new phase, a theoretical calculation was performed to find the possible structures as shown in Figures S1–S4 and Table S1 (Supporting Information). According to the simulation results (Figures S1–S4, Supporting Information), the 1D structure of $\mathrm{PFAPbI}_{2}{\mathrm{Cl}}$ is more stable than the 2D structure of $(\mathrm{PFA})_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ due to its lower intrinsic formation energy, lower reaction energy, and enhanced charge transfer, as evidenced by DFT results. This is accompanied by more stable total energy obtained by the ab initio molecular dynamics (AIMD) simulations. Altogether, these simulations strongly indicate the preferential formation of a 1D $\operatorname{PFAPbI}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}$ on a 3D $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite surface.
To provide further evidence, we conducted the XRD characterizations of the films formed with different mixing ratios of PFACl and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . The typical peak at $7.85^{\circ}$ is present in all films (Figure S5a, Supporting Information), in good consistency with the GIWAXS results (Figure 1c–d) and the XRD profiles of the PFACl-treated films (Figure S6a–b, Supporting Information). However, the counterpart films using PMACl are dominated by the 2D phase, and different PMACl proportions significantly change the number of perovskite octahedra layers (n). Among these, a pure 2D $(n=1)$ $\mathrm{PMA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{X}_{4}$ can be differentiated by a prominent peak at $5.29^{\circ}$ (Figure S5b, Supporting Information). Similarly, this 2D structure can be observed in the PMACltreated perovskite films (Figure S6c–d, Supporting Information). Therefore, the PFACl induces a 1D structure instead of a reported
2D structure perovskite (Figure S2, Supporting Information),[32] and the formation energy of the 1D $\mathrm{PFAPbI}_{2}{\mathrm{Cl}}$ is lower than the PMACl-based 2D structures, further confirming that PFACl promotes the transformation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ residues into a LD structure.
## 2.2. Surface Morphology Change
To further explore the effect of eliminating excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and examine formed LD perovskites, scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was used to detect the morphological changes of perovskite films after PMACl and PFACl post-treatments (Figure 2a). Typical of the two-step-processed perovskite films, the surface of the control film is covered by substantial $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ fragments, as seen from the top-view and cross-sectional SEM images.[14] After the posttreatment with PMACl, the amounts of these $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ fragments are partially reduced. Whereas the PFACl can thoroughly react with the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ residues and generate 1D rod-like $\mathsf{P F A P b X}_{3}$ $\ensuremath{(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{I}/\mathrm{Cl})}$ evenly distributed in the corners of adjacent grains on the 3D perovskite surface, which is consistent with the conclusion we have reached in the structural characterization in Figure 1. Furthermore, such a uniform coverage yields the surface of PFACl-treated film as more water-resistant than the control and PMACl-treated films, as reflected by increased contact angles (Figure 2b). As displayed in Figure 2c, atomic force microscopy (AFM) provides morphological observations analogous to SEM. Correspondingly, the root mean square (RMS) roughness of the control film, PMACl, and PFACl-treated films are extracted to be 36.6, 33.1, and $23.2\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Figure 2c–d). Furthermore, Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) measurements reveal a significantly reduced surface potential with a narrow distribution in the PFACl-treated perovskite film compared to the control and the PMACl-treated films (Figure 2e–f). Correspondingly, the mean work function follows an increasing tendency in the order of control, PMACl-treated, and PFACl-treated perovskite films. As such, PFACl is expected to offer enhanced capability in suppressing the charge carrier recombination at the perovskite/hole transport layer interface.[33,34] These results indicate that the PFACl post-treatment facilitates the formation of a much smoother and uniform surface of two-step processed $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite film, which would be beneficial to the interface charge extraction.[35,36]
## 2.3. Defect Passivation
The $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is considered to be the main source of the ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{0}$ , a type of recombination center, exhibiting an extremely negative effect on the performance and the long-term stability of the device.[37] Xray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were used to characterize the Pb 4f peaks of the perovskite films (Figure 3a and Figure S7, Supporting Information). The characteristic metallic ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{0}$ peaks at ${\approx}136.8\$ and $141.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be seen in the control film. More importantly, the metallic ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{0}$ was not suppressed by PMACl post-treatment but can be fully eliminated by the PFACl post-treatment, which is closely related to the fact that we observed in Figure 2, that the surface of the perovskite film is covered with 1D after PFACl treatment and the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is significantly reduced (Figure 3a). Next, the space charge-limited current (SCLC) was performed with a structure of glass/perovskite/Au to verify the trap density of different perovskite films (Figure 3b). Compared with the control film, the trap density decreases slightly from $2.65\times10^{15}$ to $2.51\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ after PMACl treatment and dramatically decreases to $1.73\times10^{15}\mathrm{{cm}^{-3}}$ after PFACl treatment. These results indicate that compared with the universal 2D perovskite passivation layer formed by PMACl, the PFACl post-treatment could significantly reduce the defects of the twostep-processed $3\mathrm{D-FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite films, such as excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , metal ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{0}$ , etc., which is conducive to the realization of efficient and stable perovskite devices.[38]
## 2.4. Photovoltaic Performance
To evaluate the photovoltaic performances of PSCs after modifications with different LD perovskites, a typical structure of glass/FTO/ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ /perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD/Au was constructed as shown in Figure 4a. The PFACl-modified PSCs demonstrate the highest PCE of $24.4\%$ , which is much higher than the PMACl-modified PSCs $(23.6\%)$ and the control PSCs $(22.8\%)$ (Figure 4b). The detailed photovoltaic parameters, the corresponding SPO, and the external quantum efficiency (EQE) are shown in Figure S8 (Supporting Information). Figure 4c shows the PCE distributions of different devices, and the statistical distributions of $J{-}V$ parameters are shown in Figure S9 (Supporting Information). It indicates the modification with PFACl can greatly improve the performance and reproducibility of the two-step-processed $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs. Further characterizations were performed to ascertain the improving effects of the LD perovskites on the device performances. The plots of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ versus light intensity curves were performed, and the slope values were calculated (Figure 4d). The PFACl-modified PSC exhibits a smaller slope value of $1.31\ k_{\mathrm{B}}T\ q^{-1}$ , compared to the other two PSCs. This result indicates that the 1D perovskite can effectively reduce trap-induced recombination, which is beneficial to improving the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF of the device.[39] The kinetics of charge transport and recombination in perovskite devices have been studied using electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). Figure 4e shows the Nyquist plots of different devices in the dark condition. The recombination resistance $(R_{\mathrm{rec}}=8.93~\mathrm{k}\Omega)$ ) of PFAClmodified devices was much higher than that of the PMACltreated $(R_{\mathrm{rec}}=7.76~\mathrm{k}\Omega)$ and the control $(R_{\mathrm{rec}}=4.35\mathrm{k}\Omega)$ devices, indicating that the carrier recombination loss in PFACl-treated device was reduced, thus improving the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of PSCs.[40] According to the Mott-Schottky relationship, the capacitance-voltage $(\mathrm{C}-$ V) curves were measured to examine the built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ of the film with the LD perovskite passivation layer (Figure 4f).
The $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ value increased from 0.90, 0.93 to $0.95\mathrm{~V~}$ for the pristine 3D, 2D/3D, and $\mathrm{1D/3D}$ devices, respectively. Therefore, the expectation of more efficient charge separation is conducive to achieving higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ , which is also consistent with the measured J–V results.[41]
In addition, we also characterized the long-term stability, thermal stability, and light stability of the unencapsulated PSCs as shown in Figure $4\mathrm{g-i}$ . The PFACl-modified PSCs remained at $\approx90\%$ of their initial PCEs after storage in ambient air $(\approx25^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ $30\pm5\%$ RH) for ${\approx}2000{\mathrm{h}}$ , which is more stable than the PMAClmodified PSCs $(\approx56\%)$ and the control PSCs $(\approx33\%)$ . The light soaking stability was performed using a white LED light in $\Nu_{2}$ for ${\approx}1000\mathrm{h}$ . The PFACl-modified PSCs also remained ${\approx}81\%$ of the initial PCEs, whereas the PMACl-modified PSCs and the control PSCs exhibited only $59\%$ and $43\%$ of their pristine PCEs, respectively. More surprisingly, even after heating at ${\approx}60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the open air $25\pm5\%$ RH) for ${\approx}500\mathrm{h}$ , the PFACl-modified PSCs also maintained ${\approx}85\%$ of their initial PCEs, while the PMAClmodified PSCs and the control PSCs severely dropped $\approx35\%$ and $48\%$ of the initial PCEs, respectively. Such outstanding enhancement of stability can be ascribed to the evenly distributed PFACl-induced 1D structure in the corners of adjacent grains and the more adequate elimination of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ residuals, which can greatly prevent erosion from the external environment factors and suppress the naked $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ -induced self-degradation of perovskites even under accelerated aging conditions.[17,42]
## 2.5. Optimization and Scalability
To further improve the perovskite film quality, a small amount of RbCl and CsI was introduced into the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite, which is known to stabilize the $\alpha$ -phase of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite and passivate bulk defects according to previous reports.[14,43,44] By implementing the post-treatment with PFACl, typical $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ residues are also converted into the 1D phase adequately (Figure S10, Supporting Information). Figure 5a shows the cross-sectional SEM image of the typical PSC with a structure of glass/FTO $/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/3\mathrm{D}$ perovskite/1D perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD/Au. Benefiting from this bulk and surface passivation, a champion PCE of $24.9\%$ with negligible hysteresis was achieved for PFACl-modified PSCs (Figure 5b). The corresponding EQE spectrum is shown in Figure 5c, which matches well with the $J{\cdot}V$ test. Additionally, we fabricated large-area $(1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2})$ devices with the PFACl modification and obtained an impressive PCE of $23.6\%$ as shown in Figure 5d. The corresponding SPO curves of the optimized smallarea and large-area solar cells are summarized in Figures S11 and S12 (Supporting Information), respectively. To further demonstrate the scalability of this LD passivation strategy, we fabricated $5\times5\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ solar mini-modules and ensured the uniformity of the perovskite films with PFACl post-treatment via a dynamic spincoating method.[45] The structures of the module and the testing mask are summarized in Figure S13 (Supporting Information). As a consequence, the solar mini-module achieved a champion PCE of $21.2\%$ with an SPO of $20.0\%$ (Figure 5e; Figure S14, Supporting Information), which is among the highest efficiency of solar mini-modules by a two-step process with an active area of over $10\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (Figure 5f), indicating that this tailored combination of bulk and surface modification is a potential strategy for achieving high-quality perovskite films in a scalable manner.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, the effects of the post-treatment with PMACl and PFACl on two-step-processed $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite films were systematically compared by tracking the alternations in dimensional, physicochemical, and photoelectric properties. Distinguished from the PMACl-induced 2D structure, a 1D structure with a lower formation energy is preferred by PFACl. More importantly, the formation of such a 1D structure is accompanied by a relatively more complete conversion of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ residual, thus contributing to lower surface roughness, narrower surface potential distribution, lower trap density, faster charge transfer, and better stability of $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite film. Thereby, a champion PCE of $24.9\%$ is achieved for the PFACl-modified $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ -based PSCs without hysteresis, and PSC with an active area of $1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ also demonstrates an impressive PCE of $23.6\%$ with negligible hysteresis. Furthermore, the large $5\times5\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ solar mini-module also obtains a champion PCE of $21.2\%$ , which is the highest result for the two-step-processed PSMs. Our results offer in-depth insights into the dimensional reduction mechanism underlying different post-treatment agents, which can pave the way for the commercial production of efficient and stable large-scale perovskite photovoltaics.
